<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1740 CE – 1757 CE · capital Pegu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mon revival kingdom in Lower Burma that captured Ava in 1752, ending the Toungoo dynasty, but fell to Alaungpaya five years later.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1740 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-people">Karen</a> and Burmese allies, rebelled against the weakened <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom</a>. They chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smim-htaw">Smim Htaw</a>, a former monk, as king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, reviving the name of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smim-htaw">Smim Htaw</a> was replaced in 1747 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binnya-dala">Binnya Dala</a>, who with French assistance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> built up an army and in 1752 captured Ava, deporting the last Toungoo king. The kingdom's base remained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> delta ports, where French and British traders competed for influence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>, a village headman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a>, raised an army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, retook Ava at the start of 1754 and swept south, capturing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-syriam">Syriam</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> in 1757. The capital was sacked and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> fled to Siam. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom.jpg" alt="Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Historic flag of Burma-Myanmar.svg: The original uploader was Renetus at English Wikipedia. derivative work: User:Zsc... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Hintar_flag_of_Burma.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1740 CE – 1757 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pegu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Smim Htaw, Binnya Dala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Restored Hanthawaddy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1748&amp;focus=restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom" aria-label="Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7301 1534 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7621 1790L7576 1622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7576 1622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7621 1790h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7621 1790h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1748&amp;focus=restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
